## Reasoning

**Entity types present.** The source is an ADS-B state-vector timeseries. Each row describes one aircraft (identified by its ICAO 24-bit transponder address `icao24`) as observed at one instant (`time`), together with its position (`lat`, `lon`), its motion (`velocity`, `heading`, `vertrate`), its altitudes (`baroaltitude`, `geoaltitude`) and broadcast labels (`callsign`, `squawk`, `onground`).

**Subjects and classes.**
- The aircraft itself: `icao24` is populated in every record and is globally unique per airframe, so `aircraft_{icao24}` is a safe subject. Class `bsm:Aircraft` (a Vehicle designed to fly), which the ontology places under Equipment/Materiel/Object/BattlespaceConcept, so it inherits `bsm:conceptName`, `bsm:alternateName`, `bsm:hasGeographicLocation`, `bsm:hasObservation`, and the unattached `bsm:hasVelocity` / `bsm:hasOrientation` / `bsm:hasAltitude`.
- Before treating the remaining columns as literals I check the object properties leaving Aircraft/Vehicle/BattlespaceConcept: `bsm:hasObservation → bsm:Observation`, `bsm:hasGeographicLocation → bsm:Location`, `bsm:Location bsm:hasGeometry → bsm:Geometry/bsm:AbsolutePoint`, `bsm:hasVelocity → bsm:Velocity`, `bsm:hasOrientation → bsm:Orientation`, `bsm:hasAltitude → bsm:Altitude`. Every one of these is evidenced by columns sitting on the same row, so each becomes its own subject rather than a literal on the aircraft: Observation (from `time`), Location + AbsolutePoint (from `lat`/`lon`), Velocity (`velocity`, `vertrate`), Orientation (`heading`), and two Altitude instances (`baroaltitude`, `geoaltitude`). Their subjects are keyed on `icao24` plus `time`, both always populated, so each timestamped state gets its own node.
- I deliberately do **not** create a `bsm:AlternateIdentifier`. That class bundles a standard code with a value, and it is only warranted when the schema names the scheme; here no column names an identifier authority, so `icao24`, `callsign` and `squawk` stay plain attributes of the aircraft.

**Near-synonym decisions.**
- `heading` vs. two candidates: `bsm:headingAngle` (Orientation — "direction the longitudinal axis points") and `bsm:absoluteBearingAngle` (Velocity — "course over ground / track angle"). Name evidence is decisive at the lexical level (the column is literally called *heading*), so I map it to `bsm:headingAngle`; I note explicitly that ADS-B feeds often publish track-over-ground under this label, so the qualifier (axis orientation vs. course over ground) is genuinely ambiguous and confidence is held down accordingly.
- `velocity` → `bsm:speedMetersPerSecond`; values ~199–244 are consistent with m/s for jet aircraft.
- `vertrate` → `bsm:inclinationAngle` is the only vertical-motion slot on Velocity, but the property is specified in degrees while the column is a rate (m/s), a unit disagreement I flag with low confidence.
- `baroaltitude` vs `geoaltitude`: the ontology's Altitude extension carries only `bsm:altitudeMeters`, with no qualifier for barometric vs geometric, so I emit two separate Altitude nodes distinguished by subject prefix rather than picking one.
- `callsign` maps to `bsm:alternateName`, whose description names "callsigns, transponder-broadcast names" verbatim; `squawk` is also a transponder-broadcast code and is attached to the same property with lower confidence.

**Unmapped.** `onground` has no fitting property: `bsm:isActiveIndicator` means "in service", not "on the ground", so forcing it would be wrong.

**IRI consistency check.** `observation_{icao24}_{time}`, `location_{icao24}_{time}`, `point_{icao24}_{time}`, `velocity_{icao24}_{time}`, `orientation_{icao24}_{time}`, `altitude_baro_{icao24}_{time}`, `altitude_geo_{icao24}_{time}` — each string is written once as a subject_transformation and copied verbatim into the referencing value's transformation. Verified character by character.

